From b5e9b3072a0df55d1ff3699677581b1c900a48d4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 19:45:05 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 02/24] Shrink the log only after a data sync covered every
 data write

The WAL, a header journal and a legacy header backup were each removed
after their own check of the latest data barrier, and a checkpoint's
salt rotation emptied the WAL after it only through an explicit check.
Every data file access now goes through one accessor that holds the
data writer's lock (a data sync outside the WAL writer's lock moved the
shared stream under a checkpoint's salt rotation), counts each write,
and records how many writes a successful data sync covered. ShrinkLog
and EmptyLog, the only ways to shrink a log recovery may still need,
stop the engine unless every counted write was covered.

Also from the review of the read-only fallback: a shared connection
reads $database from its own engine, not from the read-only snapshot
(which reported readOnly on a writable connection and could not report
readOnlyReason); index creation and rebuild on a fallback engine throw
the write error that names the refusal; a compact write no longer
retries a promotion known to be refused for every document.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UFnsmor6qyZMSd1VoKwfJh
